oboration of Johnson's slight lead would move it toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Brad Finstad (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Minnesota's 1st District in southern Minnesota leans Republican by about six points, and incumbent Brad Finstad is seeking reelection. National raters uniformly call the race Likely Republican, with two outlets rating it Lean Republican after Cook Political Report flagged the contest as more competitive in August. Democrat Jake Johnson has campaigned actively across the district's 21 counties.\n\nThe race is closer than the district's lean alone would suggest. A strong national environment for Democrats offsets much of Finstad's incumbency edge, and Johnson has matched him in fundraising, with both near $2 million raised. The only recent poll, from a Democratic-sponsored firm, showed Johnson up a point, though surveys earlier in the year favored Finstad. Prediction markets give Finstad roughly a three-in-four chance. The expected margin is modestly Republican, with meaningful uncertainty given thin polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1904, "margin": -5.38}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.19, "margin": -6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1925, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.3207, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2388, "margin": -4.0}]}